{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"527fb13a00244023","type":"group","x":-5760,"y":-960,"width":2100,"height":200,"label":"Microscopic version"},
		{"id":"8163333b7c5fc839","type":"group","x":-5760,"y":-660,"width":2100,"height":180,"label":"Macroscopic version"},
		{"id":"cb801ff251c3e2b6","type":"group","x":1600,"y":1960,"width":340,"height":230,"color":"6","label":"Electric Field Vector"},
		{"id":"df6f49546e91d9e9","type":"group","x":1520,"y":1000,"width":320,"height":220,"label":"Types of Electric Force"},
		{"id":"58fb7f623b808936","type":"group","x":-3440,"y":500,"width":300,"height":200,"label":"Types of Current"},
		{
			"id":"b8ca0956f1396408",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{B} = \\mu_{0} \\left(\\vec{J} + \\varepsilon_{0} \\frac{\\partial \\vec{E}}{\\partial t}\\right)\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4130,
			"y":-910,
			"width":440,
			"height":110
		},
		{
			"id":"8a6dfa2d63e1f768",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{H} = \\vec{J}_{f} + \\frac{\\partial \\vec{D}}{\\partial t}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4060,
			"y":-630,
			"width":300,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"d2c3f618c83003f9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Where:\n- $\\vec{E}$ = electric field\n- $\\vec{B}$ = magnetic field\n- $\\vec{D}$ = electric displacement field\n- $\\vec{H}$ = magnetic field intensity\n- $\\rho_{f}$ = free electric charge density\n- $\\vec{J}_{f}$ = free current density\n- $\\nabla \\cdot$ = divergence operator\n- $\\nabla \\times$ = curl operator",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3520,
			"y":-805,
			"width":440,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"a3e7afa47898ec9e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"1 amp equals 1 coulomb of charge moving past a point per second.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3920,
			"y":-187,
			"width":280,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"243e4024a9cdaefc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Amperes (amps) $A$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3520,
			"y":-157,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f02343275f6b3c04",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electric current requires a voltage source (electrical pressure) and a complete, closed circuit loop to flow.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3540,
			"y":50,
			"width":360,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"8a97b3b31918dac5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Commonly represented as $I$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3520,
			"y":270,
			"width":320,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"3a4ee7664b023957",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Faraday's Law of Induction==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4580,
			"y":-1380,
			"width":320,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"29163aad136c0907",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A magnetic field that changes over time induces a rotating (curling) electric field",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4580,
			"y":-1240,
			"width":320,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"7901c1422ae92a5a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==The Ampere-Maxwell Law==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4100,
			"y":-1400,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"d3d6195ec4782a68",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Magnetic fields are generated either by electric current density ($J$) or by an electric field changing over time ($\\frac{\\partial E}{\\partial t}$)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4100,
			"y":-1260,
			"width":380,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"c648267ecc629c0d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Furthermore, it showed that self-sustaining oscillating electric and magnetic fields propagate through space at the speed of light, proving light itself is an electromagnetic force.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3820,
			"y":-1755,
			"width":600,
			"height":132
		},
		{
			"id":"fc8f9ce3ed5c649c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"These equations proved that electricity and magnetism are two aspects of a single fundamental force (electromagnetism).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4390,
			"y":-1747,
			"width":480,
			"height":117
		},
		{
			"id":"6f1de6fe793c31fd",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Magnetic monopoles do not exist. Magnetic field lines are continuous loops; the total magnetic flux out of any closed surface is always zero.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5150,
			"y":-1240,
			"width":400,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"f819c013e7e06aa0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3160,
			"y":-157,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"5aeefa3be756cd2b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{H} = \\vec{J}_{f}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3430,
			"y":-1095,
			"width":280,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"6bef558fb3f504db",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Ampere's Law==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3420,
			"y":-1220,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"22dab4c638565975",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{E} = - \\frac{\\partial \\vec{B}}{\\partial t}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4560,
			"y":-905,
			"width":280,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"1fc47e21dacb6540",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{E} = - \\frac{\\partial \\vec{B}}{\\partial t}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4560,
			"y":-630,
			"width":280,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"0cc69427ab37530b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Where:\n- $\\vec{E}$ = electric field\n- $\\vec{B}$ = magnetic field\n- $\\rho$ = total electric charge density\n- $\\vec{J}$ = total current density\n- $\\mu_{0}$ = vacuum permeability\n- $\\nabla \\cdot$ = divergence operator\n- $\\nabla \\times$ = curl operator",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6360,
			"y":-710,
			"width":440,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"a02d8b5e2e64ffaa",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{B} = 0\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5080,
			"y":-895,
			"width":260,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"e73897abe31c9767",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{B} = 0\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5080,
			"y":-620,
			"width":260,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"60755f6ec6460b2a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electromagnetism==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4900,
			"y":-2040,
			"width":340,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"41f988c7269b764c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Foundation of classical electromagnetism",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4880,
			"y":-1900,
			"width":300,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"91cb8c1c01ece953",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Four Maxwell's Equations (Differential Form)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-4960,
			"y":-1720,
			"width":460,
			"height":60
		},
		{"id":"9f4c2d60508c5839","type":"file","file":"attachments/Pasted image 20260926203057.jpg","x":-5680,"y":-2296,"width":543,"height":673},
		{
			"id":"ca7ba63efaafb7e1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Gauss's Law of Magnetic Fields==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5120,
			"y":-1380,
			"width":340,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"40198a8110338736",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{E} = \\frac{\\rho}{\\varepsilon_{0}}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5640,
			"y":-905,
			"width":260,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"f9da1d85385f8c81",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\vec{D} = \\rho_{f}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5650,
			"y":-620,
			"width":280,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"6840973c75f7e11c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electric charges create diverging electric fields. Net electric flux out of a closed surface is proportional to the enclosed electric charge density ($\\rho$) divided by the permittivity of free space ($\\varepsilon_{0}$)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5760,
			"y":-1260,
			"width":480,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"6bd7583578235311",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Gauss's Law for Electric Fields==",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5660,
			"y":-1380,
			"width":380,
			"height":60
		},
		{"id":"367ea13768e018c6","type":"file","file":"attachments/del operator (nabla).jpg","x":-6700,"y":-1560,"width":720,"height":720},
		{
			"id":"f12af2c97b0026cb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Microscopic version",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-5640,
			"y":-1100,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"a488ba8d89bfeeee",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In the quantum field theory, it is called a **field excitation** or **quantum**.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1160,
			"y":1190,
			"width":380,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"d2b5f188376d3444",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**The Near-Field** is the localized, non-propagating cloud of electromagnetic energy immediately surrounding a charged particle or a magnet.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-970,
			"y":1460,
			"width":380,
			"height":170
		},
		{
			"id":"99e0671db9687d73",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Coulombs $C$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":600,
			"y":13,
			"width":210,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"af4ba51e8a2a9d48",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Properties of Charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":170,
			"y":260,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"fe950b9e623c28b0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Charge is quantized.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":600,
			"y":240,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f8e1d274febf81cb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The magnitude of the charge is independent of the type.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":600,
			"y":400,
			"width":520,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"65ab50771e2ed9e4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":175,
			"y":8,
			"width":245,
			"height":65
		},
		{
			"id":"e3f54ee7041f4cc2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The \"Local\" Field is the localized disturbance/excitation/ripple that is generated by a particle.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-900,
			"y":950,
			"width":380,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"1c48ad5d1ea7c6dc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In the classical electrodynamics, it is divided into **Near-Fields** and **Far-Fields**.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-710,
			"y":1200,
			"width":320,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"7799434ebb6dee8b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**The Far-Field** is the ripples that break away from the particle and travel through the background field across the universe as electromagnetic radiation (light, radio waves).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-680,
			"y":1700,
			"width":380,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"44a4fac076d12657",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"A single charged particle is the source of an electric field $\\vec{E}$ that extends through space in all directions, regardless of whether a second charge is present.\n\nHaving a net charge dictates how a particle responds to and creates electromagnetic fields.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-637,
			"y":220,
			"width":435,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"8fe20b8b53734418",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Conduction Electrons== are free or delocalized electrons within a solid material that can move easily and carry an electric current.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-610,
			"y":-1015,
			"width":380,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"3c34323da40e6145",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Commonly represented as rho $\\rho$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":670,
			"y":-1300,
			"width":340,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"5022d859808c225a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Commonly represented as sigma $\\sigma$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":670,
			"y":-1030,
			"width":340,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"dbfa2967838af430",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Conductivity can be thought of as a measure of how easily the conduction electrons move through a material.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":640,
			"y":-710,
			"width":340,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"1c17c6751639d046",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":730,
			"y":-860,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"1217f94900cf2ebb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Law of Conservation of Charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":600,
			"y":580,
			"width":420,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"3ce8dda98aa2538e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electrical Conductivity== is a material's intrinsic property to conduct an electric current.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":30,
			"y":-1060,
			"width":460,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"f031b6825ba0b607",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Conduction electrons can be thought of as physical vehicles that carry the electric charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":100,
			"y":-700,
			"width":320,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"c3e9d7497076e16a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electrical Resistivity== is a material's intrinsic, fundamental property of a specific material itself. It measures how strongly a substance resists current regardless of its shape or size.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":30,
			"y":-1345,
			"width":460,
			"height":150
		},
		{
			"id":"20475392f008a131",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Types of Charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":178,
			"y":-307,
			"width":240,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"e09403b9c5622675",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrical resistance is the mathematical reciprocal and opposite of electrical conductance. Meaning, they are inversely proportional.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-550,
			"y":-1600,
			"width":460,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"3f974354b2f5140a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\nG = \\frac{1}{R}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-905,
			"y":-1540,
			"width":170,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"7c736e7f825e76a1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-680,
			"y":-2140,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"f92fcf389ab853e8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"ohm $\\Omega$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1010,
			"y":-2300,
			"width":130,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"a1e25a31ef08aab3",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electrical Resistance== measures how much a specific object opposes the flow of electric current. It depends on the material and the physical dimensions (length and thickness) of the object.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-270,
			"y":-1940,
			"width":600,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"f74303dd64e81090",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Ohm's Law== states that the electric current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage applied across it, provided its temperature remains constant.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-180,
			"y":-2780,
			"width":460,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"6bdc22a94edf026b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\nV = IR\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-40,
			"y":-2520,
			"width":180,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"2bdcc77491550e28",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In the *classical electrodynamics*, it is called the **background field** or **medium**.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1720,
			"y":1170,
			"width":425,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"b2a8a94540481bd0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In the *quantum field theory*, it is called **unexcited field** or the **vacuum state**.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2160,
			"y":1170,
			"width":340,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"e24a46f7d52ab882",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The \"Global\" Field is the infinite, underlying fabric of the universe when it is at its lowest possible energy level. It fills all space and time but has an average value of zero.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2030,
			"y":950,
			"width":560,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"a4d3cc9da1de1292",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrical conductance and resistance are affected by a material's electron density (charge carrier density).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2100,
			"y":-1200,
			"width":445,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"2c61ffb3fd2cb36b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Drude's formula for electrical conductivity==\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\frac{ne^2 \\tau}{m}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $\\sigma$ = electrical conductivity\n- $n$ = free electron density\n- $e^2$ = elementary charge\n- $\\tau$ = relaxation time\n- $m$ = electron mass",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1106,
			"y":-1320,
			"width":371,
			"height":385
		},
		{
			"id":"62c8b8d1d97897a8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Relaxation Time== is the average time an electron can travel before colliding with an atom or defect. A higher relaxation time means electrons can travel further without bumping into obstacles - which results in higher conductance.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1240,
			"y":-840,
			"width":545,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"b88dfe684e96802d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"How Drude's formula is derived",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1720,
			"y":-840,
			"width":340,
			"height":90
		},
		{
			"id":"70402503c4844ca7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrical conductivity $\\sigma$ is directly proportional to the number of free electrons per unit volume $n$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1532,
			"y":-1200,
			"width":345,
			"height":145
		},
		{"id":"06ef4b3d449ce793","type":"file","file":"attachments/Pasted image 20260927111109.jpg","x":-3028,"y":-2480,"width":752,"height":325},
		{
			"id":"243542db4a70c655",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electromagnetic field can be described as either global or local.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1440,
			"y":660,
			"width":420,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"d44a9317e0760179",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Electric Charge - Field - Force\nA charged particle emits its own electric field. When two charged particles are near each other, their electric fields overlap and generate electric force. The electric force causes acceleration of the charged particles, which moves the two particles (away or towards each other).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-1720,
			"y":100,
			"width":765,
			"height":250
		},
		{
			"id":"53a210b2fff33b6e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrical resistance is like microscopic friction caused by metal's fixed atoms and ions in the crystal lattice. Changing the crystal lattice arrangement alters the metal's resistance.\n\nIn quantum mechanics, electrons travel through a *perfectly* arranged crystal lattice as smoothly as a wave passing through clear water. What actually causes electrical resistance is not the atoms themselves, but deviations or disruptions in that perfect lattice which scatter the electrons.\n\nMechanical stress (by bend, roll, or hammering a cold metal) multiplies the number of grain boundaries (the borders where misaligned crystal structures crash into each other). Electrons constantly smash into these boundaries, causing electrical resistance to increase.\n\nAnnealing (baking a heavily stressed, high-resistance metal at a high temperature and then letting it cool slowly) wipes away the grain boundaries, effectively resetting the chaotic crystal lattice arrangement back into a large, immaculate structure. Therefore, it decreases the metal's resistance.\n\nSome metals completely switch their geometric crystal patterns at different temperatures. These allotropes have different levels of resistivity. For example, iron at room temperature has a Body-Centered Cubic (BCC) structure, but when heated past 912 degree Celsius, it rearranges its atoms into a Face-Centered Cubic (FCC) structure.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2145,
			"y":-2480,
			"width":730,
			"height":570
		},
		{
			"id":"e152e084a86a07d0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrical conductance and resistance are determined by a material's electronic structure; specifically, the availability and mobility of free electrons that can move around to carry an electric current.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2280,
			"y":-1490,
			"width":660,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"951bfea00d0bf046",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Allotropes== are different structural forms of the same chemical element in the same physical state (e.g. carbon -> graphite and diamond). Even though they consist of the exact same type of atoms, allotropes have different atomic arrangements or bonding patterns. Because of this structural difference, they often exhibit drastically different physical and chemical properties.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2980,
			"y":-2140,
			"width":655,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"e3d863f8af84331b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electrical Conductor== is an object or type of material that allows the flow of charge (electric current) in one or more directions.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2980,
			"y":-1480,
			"width":360,
			"height":150
		},
		{
			"id":"7ae7aeffe501e63c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrostatic Repulsion",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1545,
			"y":1040,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"418dd8da8350ec78",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Electrostatic Attraction",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1545,
			"y":1120,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"3ee9c95080b5e6bd",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Coulomb's Law is a fundamental physics principle that calculates the electric force of attraction or repulsion between two stationary, charged particles.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1523,
			"y":1360,
			"width":500,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"335f6ee62aaa8b64",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Commonly denoted as $F$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1535,
			"y":1260,
			"width":280,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"ad464c6869e67493",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The electric force between two charges is calculated using Coulomb's Law:\n\n$$\nF=k \\frac{|q_{1}||q_{2}|}{r^2}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $F$ = magnitude of electric force\n- $k$ = Coulomb's constant ($\\approx 9 \\times 10^9 N \\cdot m^2/C^2$)\n- $q_{1},q_{2}$ = magnitudes of the two charges (in $C$)\n- $r$ = distance between the two charges (in $m$)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2162,
			"y":1130,
			"width":480,
			"height":370
		},
		{
			"id":"12796cb50fb09bd2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Charge can neither be created nor destroyed; it can only be transferred from place to place, from one object to another.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1160,
			"y":560,
			"width":510,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"c401bc214a8e93ec",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"If two objects that have equal and opposite charges are physically close to each other, then the forces they apply on some other charged object cancel, for a net force of zero.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1160,
			"y":720,
			"width":510,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"13254e8293498b51",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The smallest charge is $e \\equiv 1.602 \\times 10^{-19} \\,\\text{C}$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1160,
			"y":170,
			"width":420,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"2b76c31c1e63b8ef",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Charge is a scalar quantity, meaning it has magnitude but no direction. The positive/negative indication only refers to algebraic polarity - they are opposite states that can cancel each other out to equal zero.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1340,
			"y":320,
			"width":480,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"8999aa1ccdb5bd5a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"(SI unit) Siemens per Meter $S/m$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1120,
			"y":-900,
			"width":360,
			"height":70
		},
		{
			"id":"c33f7d91fee0e588",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\sigma = \\frac{J}{E}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1840,
			"y":-1220,
			"width":280,
			"height":100
		},
		{
			"id":"334ec36ab747821d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\sigma = \\frac{1}{\\rho}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1420,
			"y":-1235,
			"width":240,
			"height":130
		},
		{"id":"436e65106e708188","type":"file","file":"attachments/Pasted image 20260926195315.jpg","x":1120,"y":-731,"width":516,"height":343},
		{
			"id":"35a903218240f8a0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electric Force== is the attractive or repulsive push and pull that exists between any two charged particles or objects.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":760,
			"y":1205,
			"width":260,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"a7c86e0c59516d34",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"An electric force requires an interaction between at least two charged objects ($q_{1}$ and $q_{2}$). A single isolated particle cannot exert a force on itself or \"have\" a force waiting to happen in empty space.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":330,
			"y":1500,
			"width":440,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"6498f3d412ef4d34",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\rho = \\frac{E}{J}\n$$\n\nwhere:\n- E = electric field\n- J = current density",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1400,
			"y":-1890,
			"width":260,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"01cd9bb1a7b36d91",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Microscopic formula",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":970,
			"y":-1800,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"1edff7e5c97bef1e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The formula for the resistance/resistivity of a uniform conductor",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":855,
			"y":-2210,
			"width":395,
			"height":110
		},
		{
			"id":"cb9e1df0e7fbcc60",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":760,
			"y":-1440,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"fe8e161093bc0633",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Ohm-meter $\\ohm \\cdot m$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1120,
			"y":-1440,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"0f5cd398a807c979",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\rho = R \\cdot \\frac{A}{L}\n$$\n\nwhere:\n- R = electrical resistance in ohms\n- A = cross-sectional area in square meters\n- L = length of the material in meters",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1400,
			"y":-2320,
			"width":390,
			"height":300
		},
		{
			"id":"2e3b61cadc3f840e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Method 1: Calculus (Integration)\n\nInstead of adding individual charges, you treat the object as a continuous shape made of tiny, microscopic bits of charge called $dq$. You then use an integral to sum up the infinite pieces smoothly:\n$$\nE_{total} = \\int k \\cdot \\frac{dq}{r^2}\n$$\n\nTo do this, charge density must be defined based on the layout's dimensions:\n- Linear charge density $\\lambda$: total charge divided by length\n- Surface charge density $\\sigma$: total charge divided by area\n- Volume charge density $\\rho$: total charge divided by volume",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2974,
			"y":4230,
			"width":620,
			"height":440
		},
		{
			"id":"5c3c0f81ec7638f2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Line of Charges**: if you measure a point centered above a straight line of evenly spaced charges, you only have to calculate the vertical components $E_{y}$. For every charge on the left pushing diagonally right, there is a twin charge on the right pushing diagonally left, meaning all horizontal $E_{x}$ forces wipe each other out.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3024,
			"y":3820,
			"width":520,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"abf0e45514274857",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Negative Charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":540,
			"y":-360,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"e1ebd7b10a5b64fb",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Positive Charge",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":540,
			"y":-257,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"ec5efe918fd49e7e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Calculating the Field for a Few Particles\n\nThe **Principle of Superposition** states that the total electric field at any point is simply the vector sum of the individual electric fields created by each separate charge.\n\nUse vector calculation to:\n1. Calculate the magnitude of the field from each individual charge using Coulomb's Law: $E = k \\cdot \\frac{|q|}{r^2}$\n2. Determine the direction for each field (pointing away from positive charges, toward negative charges)\n3. Break each vector into x and y components using trigs (e.g., $E_{x}=E \\cos(\\theta)$, $E_{y}=E \\sin(\\theta)$).\n4. Sum the components to find the total field ($E_{total,x}$ and $E_{total,y}$).\n5. Recombine them using the Pythagorean theorem to get the final total magnitude: $E_{total} = \\sqrt{ E_{total,x}^2 + E_{total,y}^2 }$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1944,
			"y":2790,
			"width":740,
			"height":500
		},
		{
			"id":"2d826c5a8a980dc2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Calculating the Field for Common Layouts\n\nIf the nearby particles are arranged in common geometric patterns, **symmetry** often allows tedious vector math to be bypassed because opposing components naturally cancel out.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1944,
			"y":3580,
			"width":740,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"7e6fcece66cc621e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Calculating the Field for Infinitely Many Particles\n\nWhen there are too many particles to count, it becomes impossible to sum them up one by one. The calculation changes from addition to calculus, or shifts to a completely different law.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1944,
			"y":4560,
			"width":740,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"e701d5e56a35efde",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Method 2: Gauss's Law\n\nFor truly infinite systems with high symmetry, calculus can be bypassed using Gauss's Law. Instead of tracing lines from every single piece of charge, you wrap the entire infinite object in an imaginary 3D shape called a \"Gaussian Surface\" (like a cylinder or a box) and look at the total electric field poking through it.\n\nGauss's Law turns complex, infinite integrals into simple algebraic formulas:\n- Infinitely long wire $E = \\frac{2k\\lambda}{r}$\n- Infinite flat sheet $E = \\frac{\\sigma}{2\\varepsilon_{0}}$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2974,
			"y":4720,
			"width":620,
			"height":400
		},
		{
			"id":"64190d4445779c13",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Electric Dipole of Two Equal and Opposite Charges**: if you measure the field along the bisecting centerline between a $+q$ and $-q$ charge, the vertical (or horizontal) components of their fields pull and push in opposite directions, completely canceling each other out. The total field points purely horizontally (or vertical), simplifying the math significantly.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3024,
			"y":3280,
			"width":520,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"176aa9a5342897d8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\nE_{net} = \\sum \\frac{kQ_{i}}{r_{i}^2}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2798,
			"y":2980,
			"width":266,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"992a25886a3e4b0e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Symmetric Square of Charges**: imagine four identical positive charges placed at the corners of a square. Because every corner charge pushes toward the center with equal force from opposite sides, all the vector components cancel out perfectly. The total electric field at the exact center is zero.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3024,
			"y":3560,
			"width":520,
			"height":220
		},
		{"id":"b39def9418545e8a","type":"file","file":"attachments/electric_dipoles.svg","x":3764,"y":3020,"width":760,"height":760},
		{
			"id":"b6e596c4a4ef764b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Calculating the total electric field depends on whether the system consists of a few isolated charges, structured layouts, or an infinite distribution of charges.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":976,
			"y":2900,
			"width":511,
			"height":140
		},
		{"id":"8883e19e8d2b0786","type":"file","file":"attachments/Pasted image 20260927122156.jpg","x":104,"y":3360,"width":1160,"height":653},
		{
			"id":"5547e653c669ef88",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Formula",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1030,
			"y":2600,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"efba1c96b29f068f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"$$\n\\vec{E} = \\frac{\\vec{F}}{q}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1510,
			"y":2640,
			"width":223,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"ac7024311ba31ad2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The magnitude and direction of the electric field describes the electric force per unit charge that a positive test charge would experience at that specific point in space.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":980,
			"y":2000,
			"width":443,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"3d550ec7e657e6e0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Unit of Measurement",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":992,
			"y":2310,
			"width":420,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"81a849551b7830f5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Newtons per Coulomb $N/C$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1562,
			"y":2250,
			"width":320,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"df79a24f691fa124",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Magnitude",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1638,
			"y":2000,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"d06bf63150ec29f2",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Direction",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1637,
			"y":2080,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"6d7604ebf9dbfd6b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Volts per Meter $V/m$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1562,
			"y":2370,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"17e8aced77633015",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electric Field== is an invisible region surrounding a charged particle or object where other electric charges experience a non-contact force (electric force).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":60,
			"y":2340,
			"width":490,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"dfd8e3cbb6502a9c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"By universal convention in physics, the direction of an electric field is always defined by the direction of the force it would exert on a hypothetical positive \"test\" charge.\n\nBecause like charges repel and opposite charges attract, this creates two simple rules.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2153,
			"y":2260,
			"width":472,
			"height":260
		},
		{
			"id":"0f2e3d043dcdc49a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"For a positive charge $+q$, the electric field points radially outward, away from the particle in all directions.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2798,
			"y":2240,
			"width":435,
			"height":110
		},
		{
			"id":"267cba67cee5290b",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"For a negative charge $-q$, the electric field points radially inward, toward the particle from all directions.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2798,
			"y":2400,
			"width":435,
			"height":120
		},
		{"id":"cb008f77cfeb2af3","type":"file","file":"attachments/EF_positive_charge.svg","x":3380,"y":2160,"width":200,"height":200},
		{"id":"36261970f8eaa98f","type":"file","file":"attachments/EF_negative_charge.svg","x":3380,"y":2440,"width":200,"height":200},
		{
			"id":"9536c091895da17c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The **electric field of a source charge** is calculated using Coulomb's Law:\n\n$$\nE = k\\frac{\\left| Q \\right|}{r^2}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $E$ = magnitude of the electric field (in $N/C$)\n- $k$ = Coulomb's constant ($\\approx 9 \\times 10^9 N \\cdot m^2/C^2$)\n- $q$ = the charge of the source particle\n- $r$ = the distance from the charged particle to a specific coordinate.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2144,
			"y":1650,
			"width":490,
			"height":380
		},
		{
			"id":"0ae56bbb8c8e8f50",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The Lorentz Force Law (electric component) ties the electric force, electric field, and electric charge together:\n$$\nF = Q_{2} \\cdot E_{1} = Q_{2} \\cdot \\left( \\frac{kQ_{1}}{r^2} \\right) = \\frac{kQ_{1}Q_{2}}{r^2}\n$$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2880,
			"y":1480,
			"width":580,
			"height":220
		},
		{
			"id":"f354f4d93708144c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Electrostatic Equilibrium\n\nThe electric force $\\vec{F}$ on a third charge $q_{3}$ is related to the net electric field $\\vec{E}$ at that specific point by the equation:\n$$\n\\vec{F} = q_{3}\\vec{E}\n$$\n\n- If the net electric field $\\vec{E}$ at a particular point in space is **zero** (0), then multiplying it by any third charge $q_{3}$ results in a net electric force $\\vec{F}$ of **zero** (0).\n- This location is also known as a point of **electrostatic equilibrium** for the third charge.\n- The location where $\\vec{E}=0$ depends only on the two original source charges. The magnitude and sign of the third charge $q_{3}$ do not change the geometric position of this zero-field point.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":3620,
			"y":1340,
			"width":540,
			"height":500
		},
		{
			"id":"85b0b6cab80381e5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Finding the Point of Electrostatic Equilibrium\n\n| Charge Types     | Location of Static Equilibirum        | Mathematical Setup (Solving for $x$)                                                  |\n| ---------------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Like Charges     | Between the two charges               | $\\frac{\\sqrt{ \\left\\|q_{1}\\right\\| }}{x} = \\frac{\\sqrt{ \\left\\|q_{2}\\right\\| }}{d-x}$ |\n| Opposite Charges | Outside, closer to the smaller charge | $\\frac{\\sqrt{ \\left\\|q_{1}\\right\\| }}{x} = \\frac{\\sqrt{ \\left\\|q_{2}\\right\\| }}{d+x}$ |\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":4340,
			"y":1540,
			"width":840,
			"height":320
		},
		{
			"id":"b55b4f05b95c6bfd",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Direct Current (DC)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3420,
			"y":520,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"efaf4fade959011d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Alternating Current (AC)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3420,
			"y":600,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"d3a1f1530dbf0f81",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electric Current== is the rate of flow of electric charge past a specific point in a conductor or circuit.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2340,
			"y":-200,
			"width":440,
			"height":160
		},
		{
			"id":"7b12c7b4bbf6c131",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==Electric Charge== is a fundamental physical property of matter that allows particles to experience and exert electric forces on one another.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-860,
			"y":-360,
			"width":380,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"52ba99657dc187c9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"==current density== is the amount of electric current flowing through a specific cross-sectional area.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-2900,
			"y":890,
			"width":420,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"13dc8d6338ad9701",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Represented by $J$",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3720,
			"y":860,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"620dfbd2c16d5021",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Current Density is defined mathematically as the current flowing per unit area perpendicular to that flow. It bridges the gap between current and area vectors through the dot product:\n$$\nI = \\vec{J} \\cdot \\vec{A}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $I$ = current\n- $\\vec{J}$ = current density\n- $\\vec{A}$ = Area",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3640,
			"y":1045,
			"width":480,
			"height":370
		},
		{"id":"ed641d1f92099f07","type":"file","file":"attachments/full_size_version_of_the_image.jpg","x":-4040,"y":1520,"width":400,"height":320},
		{
			"id":"02a9c6a318fa340a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Microscopic Form of Ohm's Law\n\n$$\n\\vec{J} = \\sigma \\vec{E}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $\\vec{J}$ = current density vector\n- $\\sigma$ = conductance\n- $\\vec{E}$ = electric field vector",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3560,
			"y":1520,
			"width":600,
			"height":300
		},
		{
			"id":"9807a03f68cb4140",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"##### Macroscopic Form of Ohm's Law\n\nThe macroscopic Ohm's Law $V=IR$ can be derived from the current density form by considering a straight wire of length $L$ and cross-sectional area $A$:\n1. Current and Current Density: Total current density times area ($I=JA$, so $J=I/A$).\n2. Voltage and Electric Field: The voltage across the wire is the electric field times length ($V=EL$, so $E=V/L$).\n3. Substitution: Substituting these into $J=\\sigma E$ gives $\\frac{I}{A}=\\sigma\\frac{V}{L}$.\n4. Rearranging for Voltage: Solving for voltage gives $V=I\\left( \\frac{L}{\\sigma A} \\right)$. Since resistance is defined as $R=\\frac{L}{\\sigma A}$ (or $R=\\frac{\\rho L}{A}$), this simplifies to $V = IR$.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-3590,
			"y":1920,
			"width":620,
			"height":460
		}
	],
